<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="sso"
      updated="August 18, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"sso-login", "Setting up SSO login"},
        {"operations", "Common provider operations"},
        {"providers", "Provider guides"},
        {"generic-oidc", "Any other OIDC provider"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"},
        {"security-notes", "Security notes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="sso">
        <:dek>
          On Team and Enterprise plans, your team can use its own identity provider to sign in to
          emisar. Supported options include Google Workspace, Okta, Microsoft Entra, JumpCloud,
          Keycloak, and any compliant OIDC provider.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This page explains how SSO works in emisar and sets up the connection. Provisioning and
        deprovisioning from the directory is a separate piece that builds on it: <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >directory sync (SCIM)</.link>.
        Both are managed from <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on</strong>
        in your emisar workspace, and the
        <a href="#providers" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">provider guides</a>
        below walk each console step by step.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso-login">Setting up SSO login</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Two things have to exist: an OAuth application at your identity provider, and a connection
        in emisar pointing at it. You need an emisar owner or admin, plus enough access at the
        provider to create an app. Open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/sso/new"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</a>
        and choose the provider type. The form names the exact admin-console path. The
        <a href="#providers" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">provider guides</a>
        below show every screen with screenshots. <.plan_note tier={:team} />
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        At the provider, create a
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">confidential web application</strong>
        — one with a client secret. A public or single-page client will not work, because emisar
        authenticates with that secret. Copy emisar's redirect URI from the form. It ends in
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso/callback</code>
        on your emisar host. emisar always sends that exact URI and never reads one from the
        request. Paste it exactly. If the provider offers <strong class="text-zinc-200">DPoP</strong>,
        leave it off. emisar reads the ID token and never calls an API with the access token.
        Sender-constrained tokens add no value here and break the token request.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Then come back to emisar and fill in the connection. It has four sections, and the rest of
        this section explains them.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="connection-fields">Provider and OIDC connection</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">provider type</strong>
        selects the preset. The preset chooses the identity claim and applies provider-specific
        behavior. Choose the actual provider instead of Generic OIDC. The
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">display name</strong>
        appears on the sign-in page. Use the
        name your members say aloud, such as “Acme Okta”.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">issuer URL</strong>
        is critical. emisar fetches its OIDC discovery document and derives every other endpoint.
        Do not enter authorization, token, or JWKS URLs. The issuer must use HTTPS and be reachable
        from emisar. Its required format depends on the provider. The form shows the correct format
        after you choose a type, including an Okta organization, Keycloak realm, or Entra tenant
        URL.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">client ID</strong>
        and <strong class="text-zinc-200">client secret</strong>
        come from the application. The secret is write-only. The control plane stores it
        server-side, hides it in the interface, and never shows it again. It must replay the secret
        on every sign-in, so it cannot store only a hash. Protecting it at rest is an infrastructure concern. Leave
        the field blank during a later edit to keep the current secret.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">identifier claim</strong>
        permanently binds an identity. emisar offers only claims each provider issues:
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
        for most providers and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
        for Microsoft Entra. Leave
        this setting unchanged. This claim
        <a href="#subject-not-email" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          identities are matched on
        </a>
        instead of email. Your directory must send the same value as
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        ><code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code></.link>
        Then sign-in and directory sync identify one person.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="provisioning-fields">Who gets in, and what they get</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">New-user provisioning</strong>
        controls a first sign-in when no emisar member exists.
        <span class="text-zinc-300">Auto-provision</span>
        creates the member immediately. <span class="text-zinc-300">Manual</span>
        creates a request for administrator approval. Use
        Auto-provision when the provider application is assigned only to approved users. This is
        the usual setup. Use Manual when you cannot control assignments that tightly.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Turning on
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/scim"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          directory sync
        </.link>
        overrides this setting. The directory decides who exists. A sign-in from an unprovisioned
        account waits for approval, regardless of the selected setting.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Default role for new users</strong>
        is where a newly provisioned member lands: viewer, operator, billing manager, or admin.
        Provisioning never grants <span class="text-zinc-300">owner</span>
        — that stays a deliberate human grant, so no provider misconfiguration can mint one. If you
        map directory groups to roles, the mapping wins and this is only the fallback for someone
        in no mapped group.
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Roles</.link>
        explains what each one can do.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Default runner access</strong>
        controls which hosts a new member can see and use.
        <span class="text-zinc-300">No runners</span>
        is the safe default. New members can read the
        console but cannot use any runner. Grant access deliberately.
        <span class="text-zinc-300">All runners</span>
        includes current and future runners. <span class="text-zinc-300">Selected runners</span>
        limits access to named runner groups or
        hosts. Roles and runner access are independent. An operator with no runners cannot dispatch
        actions. Use that state until you grant a fleet.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Allowed email domain</strong>
        is optional. It refuses a sign-in whose provider-verified address is on another domain.
        Leave it blank to accept any address the provider returns. Either way an address your
        provider does not mark as verified is never trusted.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="activation-fields">Security and activation</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        One checkbox says <em>sign-in through this provider satisfies the account's 2FA
        requirement</em>. Enable this setting only when the provider enforces MFA. emisar then
        accepts the provider challenge instead of requesting another factor. If the provider does
        not enforce MFA, members can bypass the account requirement. The
        <a href="#sso-mfa" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">section below</a>
        covers how that interacts with the rest of the account.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Before saving, press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong>. emisar checks
        OIDC discovery and shows the authorization, token, and JWKS endpoints. A typo or unreachable
        provider fails before your team's first sign-in. Then select
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled</strong>
        and save. The connection cannot accept
        sign-ins until it is enabled. Disable it to park the connection without deleting it.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="signing-in">How members sign in</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        On the sign-in page, members select
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">“Continue with single sign-on.”</strong>
        This opens <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso</code>. They enter the
        team's address, choose the provider, authenticate at the IdP, and return signed in. An
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">allowed email domain</strong>
        checks the returned address. A
        login is refused when its provider-verified domain does not match.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="subject-not-email">
        Identities are matched by the IdP's subject, never by email
      </.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An SSO login binds to its provider and stable subject identifier. That identifier is the
        configured <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
        claim or another
        configured <strong class="text-zinc-200">identifier claim</strong>. Email addresses are
        never used for this binding. Matching by email creates an account-takeover risk, so emisar
        does not do it. A just-in-time SSO user is a separate principal, not a merge with an account
        that shares an email. If an email collides, sign-in is refused. An administrator must link
        the identity.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">First login provisions the user</strong>
        at the connection's default role (with the directory as the email authority).
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="sso-mfa">SSO sessions and your MFA requirement</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each connection has a toggle named <em>“Sign-in through this provider satisfies the
        account's 2FA requirement.”</em>
        When enabled, an SSO session satisfies the account
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          requires MFA
        </.link>
        without another emisar TOTP prompt. The identity provider enforces MFA. Disable it when
        emisar must still challenge. Magic-link sign-in still receives the usual 2FA challenge.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="operations">Common provider operations</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_h3 id="require-sso">Require SSO for the account</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        First complete a real sign-in through the connection in another browser. The form's
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong>
        checks discovery and endpoint
        reachability. It does not prove user assignment, callback acceptance, or required claims.
        Keep an owner session open
        while you test. Then open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/team"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Team → Security</a>
        and turn on <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require single sign-on</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Once required, a magic-link session cannot enter this account: emisar sends the member
        through one of this account's enabled providers on their next navigation. The requirement
        cannot be enabled with no active connection, and emisar refuses to disable or delete the
        last active connection while the requirement remains on. Turn off Require SSO before
        retiring that last connection.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        It gates account browser access and new OAuth consent for every member, including owners.
        Bearer credentials have separate lifecycles. Existing agent keys, audit-export tokens, SCIM
        bearers, and OAuth access tokens keep working. Requiring SSO revokes none of them. See
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/authentication" <> "#enforcement"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >what Require SSO covers</.link>
        for the full boundary.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="rotate-secret">Rotate a client secret</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Create the replacement secret at your IdP, paste it into the connection, and save. The
        secret is write-only, so leaving the field blank keeps the existing value. Do not delete
        the old IdP secret until a new sign-in succeeds through the saved connection. Use
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/credentials" <> "#idp"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Rotate and revoke credentials</.link>
        for every emisar credential. If you suspect a leak,
        follow
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-incidents" <> "#idp"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Security incidents</.link>
        instead.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="move-provider">Move to another issuer or client</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        After a connection has signed someone in, its issuer, client ID, and identifier claim are
        fixed. Those fields define what a stored subject means. Changing them can bind an existing
        identity to another person. To move tenants, issuers, or clients, add a new
        connection and migrate assignments to it. Client-secret rotation stays available on the
        original connection.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="disable-provider">Disable or delete a connection</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Disabling stops new sign-ins but keeps the configuration so you can enable it again.
        Deleting retires the connection and dismisses any manual-approval requests still waiting on
        it. Both actions revoke active sessions authenticated through identities on that
        connection. They do not delete users or audit history.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An account can have several provider types, but only one enabled connection of each type.
        Only one enabled emisar connection can use a given email domain. Remove or disable the
        existing domain before assigning it elsewhere.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="providers">Provider guides</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each guide walks one provider end to end — sign-in first, then directory sync — with
        screenshots of the console screens along the way.
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li id="okta" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/okta"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Okta</.link>
            — an OIDC web app for sign-in and a separate SCIM app for the directory.
            Verified against a live Okta Integrator org on August 18, 2026.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="entra" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/entra"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Microsoft Entra</.link>
            — an app registration with the
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
            claim, and an enterprise application for provisioning.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="jumpcloud" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/jumpcloud"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >JumpCloud</.link>
            — one custom application carries sign-in and provisioning together.
            Verified against a live JumpCloud tenant on August 18, 2026.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="keycloak" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/keycloak"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Keycloak</.link>
            uses a confidential client with PKCE and has no outbound SCIM.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="google-workspace" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/google-workspace"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Google Workspace</.link>
            uses an Internal OAuth client with a locked, prefilled issuer
            and no outbound SCIM.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="generic-oidc">Any other OIDC provider</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The named providers above are presets over one standard flow, not separate integrations.
        A provider that meets the contract below works through
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Generic OpenID Connect</strong>
        — including Auth0, Ping, OneLogin, Authentik, or an in-house IdP. This connection uses only
        the OIDC authorization code flow. It does not accept SAML.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You need three things from your provider, and emisar derives the rest:
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            Use a <strong class="text-zinc-200">confidential web client</strong> with a client
            secret. Register emisar's callback as a redirect URI. Public or SPA clients do not work
            because emisar authenticates with the secret.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            The <strong class="text-zinc-200">issuer URL</strong>
            must serve <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>.
            emisar fetches discovery from that address. Enter no other endpoint. The URL must use
            HTTPS and be reachable from emisar.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            Provide the <strong class="text-zinc-200">client ID and secret.</strong>
            emisar accepts
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">client_secret_post</code>
            or <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">client_secret_basic</code>, based
            on what the provider advertises.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h3 id="oidc-contract">OIDC contract</.docs_h3>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Part</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">What emisar requires</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Discovery</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The issuer must use HTTPS and have a valid OpenID Provider Configuration. Its
                authorization, token, and JWKS endpoints must also use public HTTPS. Private,
                loopback, and cloud-metadata targets are refused.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Authorization</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The provider must support authorization code flow with PKCE. The system uses S256
                when the provider advertises it. It accepts plain only when the provider advertises
                plain as its PKCE method. The request includes <code>openid</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>profile</code>, <code>state</code>, and <code>nonce</code>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Client authentication</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                A confidential client using <code>client_secret_post</code>
                or <code>client_secret_basic</code>. Public clients and signing-key client
                authentication are not supported.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">ID-token claims</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The ID token must contain a stable <code>sub</code>
                and an <code>email</code>
                marked by <code>email_verified: true</code>. It can also contain <code>name</code>. emisar reads
                these claims from the ID token and does not call UserInfo. The Entra preset uses
                <code>oid</code>
                instead of <code>sub</code>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Validation</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The callback must pass state, nonce, PKCE, ID-token signature, exact issuer,
                audience, and expiration checks before emisar resolves an identity.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keep <strong class="text-zinc-200">DPoP</strong>
        off unless your provider requires it. emisar
        reads only the ID token and never uses the access token to call an API. Sender-constrained
        tokens add no protection here and break the token request. Keep the identifier claim on <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>. Microsoft Entra needs
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
        instead, as its
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/entra"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >guide</.link>
        explains.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong> runs a real discovery request. It
        shows the authorization, token, and JWKS endpoints. A wrong issuer fails before you save or
        anyone signs in.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Use these checks for common sign-in symptoms. If the symptom is somewhere else in the
        product, start from <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/troubleshooting" <> "#identity"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >troubleshooting</.link>, which routes each one to its owner page.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Symptom</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Check</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Test connection cannot load discovery
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Use the issuer, not an authorization or token URL. Confirm it serves
                <code>/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>
                over public HTTPS and does not
                publish private or non-HTTPS endpoints.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                The IdP rejects the callback or token exchange
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Compare the redirect URI byte for byte, confirm the app is a confidential web
                client, and replace an expired client secret. For a generic provider, confirm a
                PKCE method and one of the two supported secret-auth methods are advertised.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Authentication succeeds but emisar refuses the identity
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Inspect the ID token. It must contain the configured stable identifier and a
                verified email. UserInfo-only email or profile claims are not read. If an existing
                emisar user has that email, an administrator must link the provider identity
                deliberately. emisar never merges by email.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Members loop back to SSO after Require SSO
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Use the owner session you kept open to turn off Require SSO, then complete a real
                provider sign-in before enabling it again. If no owner session remains, contact <a
                  href={EmisarWeb.MailTo.support()}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >support</a>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                SSO and SCIM create different identities
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Make SCIM <code>externalId</code>
                equal the OIDC identifier claim. Entra needs <code>objectId</code>
                on the SCIM side and <code>oid</code>
                on the OIDC side.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="security-notes">Security notes</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every SSO sign-in lands in the
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >audit log</.link>
        with its authentication method. One query shows who acted and how they signed in.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
